= Introduction and Methodological Context

This document outlines a doctoral research proposal as an experiment in Binitarian Theology. Situated at the intersection of systematic dogmatics, historical theology, and constructive mythopoesis, the proposed thesis aims to explore the following theological tenets:

- Binitarianism: Father and Son, with Son = Logos = Spirit (à la Pneumatomachians).

- An eschatological Christology where "Christ is primarily in the future, not the past."

- An "under construction" Christology akin to the Augustinian _totus Christus_ eschatology, where the _Shepherd of Hermas_ tower metaphor is applied to Christ instead of the Church.

We propose a foundational two-part approach:

- Part I: Integration of as much of the above theology as possible within a strict Calvinist context, yielding a "Five-Point Eschatological Binitarian Calvinism."

- Part II: Giving free rein to the above theology beyond orthodox Calvinism, within a renewed mythical origin story centered on the novel theonyms Tukamo (the Father) and Kusapo (the Son-Logos-Christ-Spirit). These names were chosen at random to emphasize the break with the deadwood accreted around the Christian tradition. The goal is nonetheless to keep as much of the _kerygma_#footnote[The term is Bultmannian, but the approach pursued here is different. It is a re-mythologization rather than an abandonment of myth.] as possible, especially those elements found in the Pauline letters and Johannine material.

Subsequently, the theology of Kusapo and Tukamo will be used in the following way:

- Part III: Philosophy. A bespoke philosophical approach will be built, "Skeptical Pisticism"—clearly distinct from the existing umbrella term "Skeptical Fideism"—drawing heavily on the philosophy of the later Wittgenstein and the epistemology of Thomas Kuhn. This philosophical framework will be used to substantiate the claim that "Christianity = Sextus Empiricus + the Cross."

- Part IV: History. This part will trace the binitarian theological pattern from the Neo-Babylonian Marduk/Nabu pair to Calvin's reticence about conciliar Trinitarian language, with the following intermediary steps: the tension between the binitarian Marduk priesthood and Sin-centric monolatry under Nabonidus, the Jewish "Two Powers in Heaven" tradition, and the Islamic Allah/_Haqiqat al-Muhammadiyya_ with its Sufi and eschatological manifestations.

Finally, we will conclude with a few remarks on roads not taken and a possible road ahead: how renewed Binitarianism could help us defeat the demon-worship of our time: Nietzschean-existentialist-PoMo seductive monster idolatry (Heathcliff, Nikolai Stavrogin, Jeffrey Epstein, Christian Grey).

= Part I: Orthodox Binitarian Eschatological Calvinist Theology

This section explores Binitarianism within the context of a strict adherence to Calvinist orthodoxy. The goal is to determine whether this is possible without doing excessive violence to this theological context. A subsidiary goal is to explore a possible alternative outcome of the 1537 debate with Pierre Caroli: What if Calvin had chosen to adopt a Pneumatomachian position and said, "There are only two persons in the Binity: the Father and the Son, the latter being called Jesus Christ or the Logos or the (Holy) Spirit" (irrespective of the political fallout, of course)? Accordingly, this part will endeavor to rigorously define the parameters of "Orthodox Binitarianism" and synthesize this framework within five-point Reformed Calvinist soteriology, specifically filtered through a deeply teleological and eschatological outlook.

== 1. The Context: Calvin's Formative Years up to the Debate with Caroli

John Calvin’s intellectual formation was deeply shaped by his humanist education, which instilled in him an uncompromising commitment to an _ad fontes_ methodology and a strict adherence to the biblical text. During his formative years, this rigorous biblicism fostered a growing uneasiness with the traditional Trinitarian vocabulary inherited from the late antique councils. Driven by a desire to speak of God strictly within the bounds of biblical revelation, Calvin initially exhibited a marked reluctance to employ non-biblical terminology such as _Trinitas_ and _Persona_. He feared that binding the faithful to words absent from Scripture might obscure the vibrant, relational God revealed in the text.

At the core of Calvin's discomfort was the Church's excessive reliance on Platonic and Aristotelian vocabulary—most notably terms like _ousia_ (essence or substance) and _hypostasis_ (subsistence or person). For the Genevan reformer, the importation of these Hellenistic metaphysical concepts opened the door to dangerous "Scholastic temptations." He recognized that mapping the living God onto rigid philosophical categories risked subjecting the divine mystery to abstract academic dissection, distracting believers from the lived, experiential reality of grace. By reducing the Godhead to speculative formulas, Scholasticism threatened to turn theology into a sterile enterprise detached from the organic language of the Gospel. This disdain for abstract philosophical theology is vividly illustrated by Calvin's vehement rejection of Duns Scotus and the intricate, hair-splitting theological distinctions of the _via moderna_, which he viewed as vain sophistry rather than authentic biblical exegesis.

An additional objective of this section is to draw a direct conceptual link between Calvin’s documented linguistic uneasiness and the historical precedent of the fourth-century AD _Pneumatomachia_ (the "Spirit-fighters"). Historically, the Pneumatomachians were condemned for refusing to define the Holy Spirit as a distinct, co-equal hypostasis, partly due to a lack of explicit biblical warrant for such non-scriptural terminology. By treating the Pneumatomachian hesitation not merely as a vanquished heresy but as a suppressed instinct to protect biblical simplicity, this proposal explores a compelling counterfactual. It posits that adopting a functionally Pneumatomachian stance—thereby constructing a robust Binitarianism free from Platonic speculation—would have perfectly aligned with Calvin's humanist drive to purge theology of its Scholastic excesses.

== 2. The Dogmatic Parameters of Orthodox Binitarianism

This section defines "Orthodox Binitarianism" as a coherent, systematic model rather than a regression to historical heresies. It structurally prioritizes the eternal, dyadic relationship between the Father and the Word-Son-Christ. The thesis argues that a two-person taxonomy offers a more coherent exegesis of the earliest scriptural data. It outlines how pneumatological functions are systematically reassigned as the active power and shared relational bond of the Father and the Son, rather than an independent third hypostasis. The boundaries of this model are delineated to ensure it maintains a high Christology and strict divine unity.

To ensure that this proposed structural shift in the Godhead does not devolve into theological incoherence or synergistic heterodoxy, the Five Points of Calvinism (Total Depravity, Unconditional Election, Limited Atonement, Irresistible Grace, and the Perseverance of the Saints) will be systematically employed as a rigid set of dogmatic guardrails. Modifying the ontology of the Trinity inherently risks destabilizing the soteriological framework that depends upon it. By strictly enforcing the operational logic of these five points, and Calvinist soteriology in general, the thesis guarantees that the Binitarian theology under development perfectly preserves the core intent, weight, and pastoral assurance of classical Reformed soteriology: the absolute, unyielding sovereignty of God in salvation.

We will show how the five points map seamlessly onto a Binitarian ontology, often yielding greater conceptual clarity than their Trinitarian counterparts. Unconditional Election and Irresistible Grace, for instance, are dynamically re-articulated as the direct, sovereign actions of the Father decreeing salvation and the Son-Logos-Christ actively executing and securing that decree within the believer as Spirit. By eliminating the necessity of a third, distinct hypostasis to mediate grace, the Binitarian model streamlines the mechanics of sovereign salvation. We will also show that this allows a more straightforward exegesis of the Pauline corpus, which has been called "binitarian" itself by some scholars (e.g., Larry Hurtado). This framing ensures that the entire soteriological arc—from predestination to final perseverance—is held firmly and exclusively within the intimate, unbroken relationship between the Father and the Son, thoroughly upholding the Reformed tradition.

== 3. Eschatological Calvinism and the Divine Decrees

The central theoretical pivot here is the "eschatological" modifier. Rather than anchoring the divine decrees solely in an inaccessible, pre-temporal past, Eschatological Binitarianism locates the ultimate meaning of predestination within the _eschaton_. To summarize: "Christ is primarily in the future, even though He is also in the extra-temporal 'past'." This forward-looking approach frames election and reprobation not as arbitrary (mysterious) historical ordinations, but as necessary, teleological components pulling creation toward the final eschatological consummation of the Father-Son dynamic. This anticipates the fully future-oriented theology of Kusapo in Part II, determining how much of it can be imported backward into a Calvinist framework without breaking it. Johannine material, and especially the concept of "the vine," will be used here primarily:

#pad(left: 2em)[
  _“I am the true vine, and my Father is the vinedresser. Every branch in me that does not bear fruit he takes away, and every branch that does bear fruit he prunes, that it may bear more fruit. [...] I am the vine; you are the branches. Whoever abides in me and I in him, he it is that bears much fruit, for apart from me you can do nothing.”_ (John 15:1-2, 5)
]


= Part II: Tukamo and Kusapo Theology

The second major division of the thesis shifts from systematic dogmatics to experimental constructive theology. It formally introduces the heuristic, archetypal concepts of Tukamo (≈ the Father) and Kusapo (≈ the Son-Logos-Christ-Spirit), establishing them as a "New Mythos"—a narrative and symbolic architecture designed to navigate the theology outlined in the previous part beyond the limits of traditional and even biblical Christianity while keeping as much of the _kerygma_ intact as possible.
#pad(left: 2em)[
  _“In the beginning was Kusapo\
  He was in the beginning with Tukamo\ 
  Everything that was made, was made for Kusapo, the vine\ 
  Because Kusapo is Tukamo at the end and in the beginning.”_ 
]

== 1. The Constructive Necessity of a New Mythos

This subsection defends the methodological use of mythopoesis in the current cultural context. It argues that purely scholastic, propositional categories frequently fail to capture the paradoxical, existential weight of Binitarian relationality and the grueling reality of Girardian mimetic desire (see section 3 below). A "New Mythos" is therefore required to translate rigid dogmatic pronouncements into a dynamic framework capable of engaging the human imagination and articulating the deeper mysteries of sovereign grace and the dialectics of revelation.

The deliberate selection of two entirely novel, randomly generated names—Tukamo and Kusapo—serves a vital pedagogical purpose. Traditional Christian terminology (such as "Father," "Son," "Logos," and "Spirit") carries an enormous semantic overload, heavily encrusted with centuries of disparate theological, cultural, and philosophical assumptions. These terms are also heavily burdened by the suspicion of hypocrisy that almost universally taints the Christian name today. Utilizing the traditional vocabulary inevitably traps both the writer and the reader in a minefield of inevitable misunderstandings, as ingrained preconceptions override the specific theology being presented, especially in a homiletic or apologetic context. By employing the theonyms Tukamo and Kusapo, the thesis forces a cognitive reset, bypassing entrenched linguistic reflexes and allowing the pure structural and ontological dynamics of the proposed Binitarian framework to be examined on their own merits without the interference of historical baggage.

== 2. Tukamo and Kusapo Theology

Here, the specific theological characteristics of Tukamo and Kusapo are systematically delineated, using both prose and free verse as in the prologue of Part II above. These terms will be deployed as experimental dialectical archetypes that conceptualize the ultimate forces operating within the Binitarian cosmos. They represent the ontological and narrative pillars of the experimental mythos, defining their metaphysical properties and their indispensable role in conceptualizing Binitarian interaction with the created order.

The theology of Kusapo, specifically, conceptualizes the Son-Logos-Christ-Spirit not merely as a static, pre-existent entity, but as a vast, eschatological reality that is fundamentally oriented toward the future. Kusapo is the ultimate teleological endpoint of the Binitarian decree, an overarching structure into which all of creation is being irresistibly drawn. In this framework, Kusapo acts as a sovereign, aggregating force, moving backward through time from the _eschaton_ to gather up "what works" from the temporal realm, where the never-ending Augustinian _aversio a Deo_ temptation lurks, and integrate it into the eternal, unshakable reality of the heavenly Kullaba. This frames the process of salvation and cosmic consummation as an ongoing, dynamic integration. At the _eschaton_ it becomes possible to state that:

#pad(left: 2em)[
  _"Because Kusapo is Tukamo at the end, He is also at the beginning.”_ 
]

Crucially, this theology posits that Kusapo is currently "under construction" within the temporal sphere, operating through the ongoing sanctification and spiritual warfare of the elect. This concept draws a direct, illuminating parallel with the early Christian apocalyptic text, the _Shepherd of Hermas_ (also known as the _Pastor of Hermas_). In Hermas's visions, the Church is depicted as a great tower currently under construction, built upon the waters and meticulously assembled from distinct, living stones (the believers) under the strict supervision of angels. Those stones that are flawed or unsuitable are cast aside, while those that fit perfectly are integrated into the seamless, singular structure of the tower. The parallels with Augustinian _totus Christus_ eschatology will be explored here.

Crucially, within this mythological framework, the Crucifixion is not conceptualized merely as a singular, unrepeatable historical anomaly. Rather, it represents an archetypal manifestation of a recurring pattern of "cathartic slimming down" spasms within Kusapo's developing body. As Kusapo grows and integrates material from the temporal realm, defective and unviable structures inevitably build up. Periodic spasms occur to ruthlessly expel this structural deadwood into the surrounding "void of death." Typically, the Christian crucifixion narrative operated precisely as one such "slimming down" of Second Temple Judaism, forcefully stripping away its particularistic accretions to forge a universalized, streamlined version of its fundamental monotheistic principles. This cosmic pattern of integration and violent shedding is fractal (Schumpeterian creative destruction), echoing across history in massive systemic resets. Other prominent examples include the K-T extinction event, the dismantling of the BMAC and Indus Valley trade-based societies by proto-Indo-Iranian warbands#footnote[See Bernard Sergent, _Genèse de l'Inde_, 1997. BMAC stands for "Bactria-Margiana Archaeological Complex," a term coined by Soviet archaeologist V. Sarianidi in the 1970s.], the Late Bronze Age collapse, the fall of the Roman Empire, and the looming civilizational crisis often termed the "Fall of the West" (or TEOTWAWKI, "The End Of The World As We Know It," in colloquial "prepper#footnote[The mostly American "prepper" communities are the millenarian sects of our time.]" terminology).

== 3. Kusapo and the Saved Believer

Within this framework, "sin" is primarily defined as everything that stems from the Augustinian _aversio a Deo_ and _conversio ad creaturam_ dynamic. The Girardian#footnote[The Girardian theory used here is limited to _Deceit, Desire, and the Novel_, as we consider the rest of René Girard's work to be mostly an unnecessary dilution of his initial insight (with some small exceptions).] triangular desire scheme is the standard understanding of idolatry used here: Idol = Mediator (_creatura_), Idolater = Desiring Subject, _Prasad_#h(.2em)#footnote[_Prasāda_ is the Hindu term for the part of the sacrificial offering consumed by the devotee after _pūjā_. This exists in all forms of idolatrous worship, including in the covert ("world of the Black") idolatry described by Girard. It exists also in Christianity as the Bread and the Wine.] = Object of Desire. The canonical Augustinian example of this is the pear theft incident in the _Confessions_, in which the "friends" are the Mediator, young Augustine is the Desiring Subject, and the pears are the Object of Desire. Furthermore, "sin" is understood here as "the curse of language": when we use language, we have to commit (make promises) in order to make cooperation possible. However, committing makes us liars because we are not _sure_ we can fulfill the promises we make. All this is understood in the context of a Wittgensteinian account of language (which we call "skeptical pisticism", see the next section). 

Within the new mythos, the saved believer occupies a specific, functional role in the ongoing eschatological development of Kusapo, operating primarily in one of two distinct modes. The first is the role of the entrepreneur, positioned "at the edge of Kusapo." This individual, far from the ego-driven Randian heroic cliché, engages in a form of exploratory self-sacrifice, venturing into the chaotic, conditional realm of worldly trial and error to discover and bring back "what works versus what doesn't" for the construction of the eschatological structure. The failures, bankruptcies, and grueling struggles of the entrepreneur are not meaningless tragedies, but essential stress tests in the construction process. They discard the unviable elements of reality so that only what is eternally robust may be integrated into Kusapo. The archetypal entrepreneurship story here is not that of Elon Musk or Jeff Bezos, but that of the Ingalls family in Laura Ingalls-Wilder's books; the story of the anonymous humble ants extending the anthill through sacrificial exploration. Failure here is not sin, in contrast to the original meaning of _hamartia_ in Koine Greek or _chatta'ah_ in Hebrew. _Conversio ad creaturam_ is the chief sin.

Alternatively, the believer may function as an aware bureaucratic-worker cog "in the middle of Kusapo." Rather than exploring the perilous frontier, this believer operates with the profound, Calvinistic assurance of salvation, recognizing their life and sanctification as a vital, pre-ordained thread subsumed into Kusapo's eschatological "mega-process,"#footnote[This utilizes an Information Technology metaphor drawn from operating systems technology: a "process" is a large, overarching program execution environment with its own memory space, whereas "threads" are smaller, individual sequences of instructions operating, generally in large numbers and in parallel, within that larger process. Thus, the individual believer operates as a thread subsumed within the grand teleological execution of Kusapo.] implementing the internal metabolism of Kusapo's body. In both modes, the cohesion of this vast structure is maintained by _agape_, which is stripped of its modern, sentimental, and pseudo-lofty (and thus prideful) delusions of "unconditional love." Instead, _agape_ is grounded heuristically as a divinely repurposed social-mammal (sheep) cohesion instinct—a fierce yet tender, practical, and enduring bond that keeps the living stones of Kusapo firmly bound together while the immense project of construction proceeds apace. The _eschaton_ (rather than the Cross) is the final triumph over sin as Girardian Metaphysical Desire (_conversio ad creaturam_) is finally vanquished and all promises are fulfilled, thus overcoming the "curse of language". Kusapo becomes coextensive with Tukamo, including all the threads of the saved believers that live "in Him".

It is absolutely paramount to clarify that this participatory model does not collapse into Arminian synergism. The believer does not cooperate with God on an equal footing to achieve salvation. The sheer scale, complexity, and teleological scope of Kusapo's construction transcend human comprehension to such a degree that the believer generally has no idea in what specific way they are contributing to the grand design. We are tools in the hands of the Builder. The only clear feedback mechanisms are failure and perseverance—such as when an entrepreneur's venture collapses and they simply conclude, "Well, that didn't work." The sovereignty of Tukamo (the Father) remains absolute, and the architecture of Kusapo is predestined; the believer merely experiences the grinding reality of being shaped and fitted into a structure they cannot fully comprehend.

== 5. Exegetical Mapping in the Johannine Corpus

Here are a few examples of what can be done within the Johannine corpus. In the verses quoted below, the Christian theonyms have been replaced by Kusapo and Tukamo, as an illustration of the continuity between Johannine theology and the experiment conducted here. The same methodology will be applied to the rest of the NT and select parts of the OT (like the Psalms).

=== 5.1 The Johannine Prologue (John 1:1-18)

We have seen earlier that a "Kusapo Prologue" can be built on the model of the first verses of the Gospel of John: 

