The final, absolute triumph of _agape_ over pride at the end of time. The illusion of separation ends, and the universe achieves total, unified cohesion.

=== 5.6 Other Exegetical Mappings

Beyond the foundational texts mapped above, the proposed research will explore several other crucial scriptural connections to validate the exegetical utility of the Tukamo-Kusapo framework. Chief among these is the narrative of the man born blind (John 9:1-41), which will be interpreted through a Wittgensteinian-Kuhnian lens as a clash of language-games. This pericope perfectly illustrates the flexibility of raw, experiential belief (the healed man's pragmatic assertion of his localized experience) triumphing over the ossified, Pharisaic dogma that refuses to assimilate new paradigms. Furthermore, recurrent Johannine passages where Jesus declares He is "going to the Father" (e.g., John 14:12, 14:28, 16:28) will be structurally reinterpreted not as a spatial ascent, but as Kusapo moving teleologically toward Tukamo alongside the arrow of time, gathering creation into the eschaton. 

= Part III: Philosophy

== 1. Skeptical Pisticism

To theoretically undergird this project, we establish a "skeptical pisticist" philosophical foundation deeply informed by the anti-epistemological frameworks of Ludwig Wittgenstein and Thomas Kuhn. Within this paradigm, traditional epistemic-gnostic vocabulary—which falsely promises access to absolute, unmediated knowledge—is strictly banished and systematically replaced by "degree of belief" reasoning. Crucially, this approach is not a retreat into irrational fideism, nor does it entail a rejection of reason. Rather, "Reason," or more accurately "Reasonableness," is redefined as the innate human ability to correctly process degree-of-belief propositions and to communicate effectively about them. Undergirding this ability is what we term the "Field of Credit"—a quasi-metaphysical abstraction encompassing the social and operational networks of reputation, businesslike trust, and creditworthiness. This conceptualization clarifies why idolatry rightly occupies the paramount position at the top of the Decalogue: idolatry is fundamentally a catastrophic pollutant of the Field of Credit. It floods the pistic environment with "counterfeit credit" artificially manufactured around each idol. In stark contrast, the ideal field of genuine credit is constructed only through the painstaking, meticulous recording of verified track records—an aggregative, historical process that is perfectly mirrored in the eschatological construction of Kusapo's body across time.

Furthermore, the strategic deployment of René Girard’s mimetic theory provides the critical tools necessary to profoundly revitalize Augustine of Hippo’s classical theology of sin. Augustine’s dichotomy of _aversio a Deo_ and _conversio ad creaturam_ (turning away from God and turning toward the creature) is re-articulated through Girardian mechanics as the tragic fall from "Vertical Transcendence" into the mimetic rivalries of "Deviated Transcendence." Building directly upon the Wittgensteinian-Kuhnian foundation established above, the thesis explicitly identifies sanctification as the grueling spiritual and operative battle against the pollution of the Field of Credit. It is the active, continuous exposure and dismantling of the mimetic illusions, a critical demystification performed by the introspective incarnation of Kusapo—the Spirit of Reliability—operating actively within the believer's mind to purge the deceptive allure of the creature.

Consequently, this skeptical pisticist foundation necessitates a decisive rejection of the classical Calvinian concept of the _sensus divinitatis_—the purportedly innate, universal human awareness of God. While historically deployed to render humanity without excuse, positing a direct, unmediated epistemic seed fundamentally violates the stricture against gnostic certainty. If all human access to reality is inherently mediated through historical, linguistic, and operational track records within the Field of Credit, then positing an unmediated, universally distributed epistemic seed of divine knowledge violates our core Wittgensteinian-Kuhnian commitment. By abandoning the _sensus divinitatis_, we similarly render obsolete the complex Scholastic Calvinist distinction between archetypal knowledge (_theologia archetypa_, God's infinite self-knowledge) and ectypal knowledge (_theologia ectypa_, the accommodated knowledge granted to finite humans). This scholastic scaffolding, constructed precisely to salvage epistemic certainty while acknowledging human finitude, becomes an unnecessary metaphysical distraction. In the Kusapo framework, we do not require a diluted "ectypal" version of absolute knowledge; instead, we rely entirely on the operational, relational reality of trust—the probabilistic "degree of belief" forged in the crucible of the construction site and validated by the historical track record of the Word. Salvation is indeed entirely by _pistis_ alone.

== 2. Christianity as "Sextus Empiricus + the Cross"

This thesis extends into a macro-historical framework by casting classical Christianity—when rigorously stripped of its Hellenistic philosophical accretions—as the ultimate culmination of the Axial-Age rejection of epistemic-centric philosophy#footnote[See attached Axial Age paper.]. As highlighted in recent historical scholarship, particularly Christopher I. Beckwith’s tracing of Pyrrhonism back to early Buddhism via Alexander the Great's Indian campaigns, the most profound intellectual breakthrough of antiquity was not the invention of speculative metaphysics, but its systematic dismantling. Both the early Buddhist wandering ascetics and the Hellenistic Pyrrhonian skeptics—and their later Roman-era descendants like Sextus Empiricus—diagnosed the human pursuit of absolute metaphysical knowledge as an engine of pointless psychological suffering. By framing the Christian revelation as "Sextus Empiricus plus the Cross," we align the radical skeptical dismantling of dogmatic certainty—the Pyrrhonian suspension of judgment (_epoché_), corresponding with the Pauline rejection of "the wisdom of the world"—with the definitive Christian solution. The Cross fills the epistemic void left by skepticism not with a new, superior philosophical architecture, but with an agonizing, somatic act of relational trust and sacrifice. We believe that this is the direction Calvin was moving in before the debate with Caroli, and other political considerations, forced him to stop short of outright skeptical pisticism.

From this Skeptical-Pisticist Christian perspective, the entire enterprise of classical Epistemological-Philosophy is systematically demystified and dismissed as mere "competence onanism." This intentionally pejorative but conceptually precise descriptor targets the psychological reality of philosophy, including the very etymology of the word itself: if _sophia_ is understood pragmatically as "competence" (its original meaning in Greek), then _philia_ reveals itself not as a noble "love," but as a base, addictive craving—structurally identical to the pathological fixations found in pedophilia or zoophilia. The philosopher is an addict craving the illusion of absolute competence. This pathological drive feeds the ego with nefarious superstitions predicated (overtly or not) on the "correspondence theory of truth," generating elaborate metaphysical ghosts (Platonic forms, Aristotelian teleologies, or Scholastic hypostases) that falsely promise unmediated access to absolute reality and hence individual autonomy. Rather than curing the mimetic fragmentation of the human mind, this intellectual onanism merely recreates the Girardian trap at a higher level of abstraction, substituting the worship of wooden idols with the equally hubristic worship of human cognition and logical syllogisms. This is substantially what Buddhism, through its doctrine of _anātman_ ("no soul" i.e. no metaphysical essence), also rejects. The critique of philosophy presented above is also somewhat similar to Al-Ghazali's _Tahāfut al-Falāsifa_#footnote[_The Incoherence of the Philosophers_ (Arabic: تهافت الفلاسفة, romanized: _Tahāfut al-Falāsifa_) by Al-Ghazali (Persian: ابو حامد محمد ابن محمد غزالی طوسی, romanized: Abū Ḥāmid Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad Ghazālī Ṭūsi, late 11th century).] in intent, if not in doctrine.

The profound epistemological humility resulting from this approach aligns perfectly with John Calvin's virulent anti-scholasticism and his core humanist instincts. Just as Calvin sought to aggressively demolish the intricate, speculative castles of the _via moderna_ and the medieval Schoolmen—which he viewed as vain, competence-driven sophistry attempting to domesticate God within human syllogisms—the "Sextus Empiricus plus the Cross" model radically reorients the believer. It actively turns the subject away from the illusory, ego-gratifying pursuit of absolute philosophical certainty and toward the experiential, pistic reality of living as an active thread within Kusapo's eschatological body. In this framework, the believer navigates reality through faithful, operational trial and error (trust) rather than arrogant, static philosophical knowledge (_episteme_, _gnosis_), perfectly echoing the Reformation's insistence on _sola fide_ over the quasi-gnosticism of the medieval intellectual elite.

The Binitarian reformulation effected here drives the above point home with renewed force, through the shedding of the Neoplatonic-stained language of traditional trinitarianism and through a re-personalization of theology around a single trust-bearer in this world: Kusapo.

= Part IV: History

In all of the following sections, we will use the Tukamo/Kusapo pair as we have done above with the Gospel of John, to reformulate one key textual passage in each particular theological tradition, demonstrating the structural continuity of this binitarian pattern.

== 1. The Marduk-Nabu pair of the early Neo-Babylonian period

The theological architecture of the early Neo-Babylonian period provides what is arguably the historical root of all subsequent binitarian manifestations in the Near East. At the apex of the pantheon stood Marduk, the supreme creator and distant king of the gods. However, the active administration of the cosmos and the execution of divine decrees were entirely delegated to his son, Nabu, the god of wisdom, writing, and prophecy. This Marduk-Nabu dyad functioned as a perfect, operational binity: the inaccessible Father who decrees, and the immanent Son (the Word/Scribe) who materializes those decrees into historical reality. This structural pairing became the inescapable theological template for the region, deeply influencing the religious imagination of all surrounding cultures.

To illustrate this using our established theonyms, we can reformulate a standard Neo-Babylonian hymn to Nabu#footnote[See, for example, the "Great Hymn to Nabu" in Benjamin R. Foster, _Before the Muses: An Anthology of Akkadian Literature_, 3rd ed. (Bethesda: CDL Press, 2005), 621.]. In this paradigm, the text reads: 

#pad(left: 2em)[
  _"O Kusapo, magnificent son, beloved of Tukamo, you are the one who holds the tablet of destinies. Tukamo decrees the fates from his unapproachable throne, but it is you, Kusapo, who writes them into the reality of the earth, bringing order to the works of men."_]

Here, the proto-Logos function of Kusapo as the active, mediating interface for the Prime Mover Tukamo is already fully articulated.

== 2. Nabonidus: Sin-centric monolatry vs. the traditional pair

The reign of the last Neo-Babylonian king, Nabonidus, represents a massive and highly instructive religious fracture. Nabonidus alienated the powerful Marduk-Nabu priesthood in Babylon by attempting to elevate the moon god, Sin, to a position of absolute, monolithic supremacy. This Sin-centric monolatry, drawing on the ancient lunar cult centers of Ur and Harran, violently clashed with the traditional binitarian model. To escape the hostile, entrenched dyadic establishment in Babylon, Nabonidus effectively abandoned his capital and relocated to the desert oasis of Tayma for a decade. This Babylon versus Tayma split physically materialized the two rival theological factions: the organic, relational binitarianism of the traditionalists against the rigid, centralizing monolatry of the reformer.

The hostility of the binitarian faction toward this monolithic disruption is perfectly captured in the _Verse Account of Nabonidus_#footnote[For a reliable translation and historical context, see A. Leo Oppenheim's translation in James B. Pritchard, ed., _Ancient Near Eastern Texts Relating to the Old Testament_, 3rd ed. (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1969), 312–315, or Paul-Alain Beaulieu, _The Reign of Nabonidus, King of Babylon 556–539 B.C._ (New Haven: Yale University Press, 1989).]. Reformulated into our mythos, the traditionalists' grievance reads: 

#pad(left: 2em)[
  _"He uttered blasphemies against Tukamo and Kusapo, neglecting their festivals and declaring, 'I shall build a temple to the One God of the Moon, and until I have achieved it, I shall halt the festivals of the city.' Thus he abandoned the dynamic order of Kusapo for a sterile, solitary idol."_]

The Babylonian elite recognized that abandoning the dyadic engine of reality meant cosmic stagnation.

== 3. Second Temple Judaism: The "Two Powers in Heaven" heterodoxy

Following the exile, this deep structural antagonism reappeared within Second Temple Judaism as the famous "Two Powers in Heaven" heterodoxy. The elevation of figures like Enoch (transformed into the supreme angel Metatron), the complex angelology of the period, and the apocalyptic vision of the "Son of Man" all served as a profound resurgence of the binitarian archetype. This rich theological milieu strongly suggests the roots of an original Alexandrian Jewish Gnosticism, wherein a lesser, active YHWH/Logos interacts with creation on behalf of the High God. Crucially, the antagonism between strict, absolute monotheists and the "Two Powers" adherents perfectly mirrors the previous Babylonian conflict. It may be understood as a deeply ingrained, post-exilic Jewish memory of the clash between the Sin-centric faction and the Marduk-Nabu priesthood. This historical connection is further corroborated by the _Prayer of Nabonidus_ found among the Dead Sea Scrolls at Qumran, and the fact that Abraham's origin story (journeying from Ur to Harran) traces a path exactly between the two major ancient cult centers of the moon god Sin, explicitly aligning the patriarchal narrative with the monolithic faction.

The enduring power of this binitarian memory is best exemplified in the Book of Daniel (7:13-14) concerning the Son of Man. Reformulated into our construct, the text reveals its underlying architecture:

#pad(left: 2em)[
  _"I saw in the night visions, and behold, with the clouds of heaven there came one like Kusapo, and he came to Tukamo and was presented before him. And to Kusapo was given dominion and glory and a kingdom, that all peoples, nations, and languages should serve him."_
]

Here, the eschatological trajectory of Kusapo gathering all temporal reality to present it to Tukamo is starkly prefigured.

== 4. From the Mishnaic rejection to the Kabbalistic return

After the destruction of the Second Temple and the concurrent rise of Christianity, orthodox Rabbinic Judaism systematically purged the "Two Powers" heterodoxy to differentiate itself from the emerging binitarianism of the early Church. This Mishnaic rejection forced the dyadic impulse underground, labeling it as a dangerous heresy. Yet, as the pioneering scholarship of Gershom Scholem meticulously mapped, these structural motifs did not perish. They survived at the esoteric fringes and in Gnostic-adjacent folk traditions, most vividly attested by the Aramaic incantation bowls of late antique Mesopotamia, which preserved the names of intermediary angels and demiurgic executors of the divine will.

This subterranean stream of binitarian mysticism gradually migrated westward along established Jewish trade and communication networks. From the rabbinic academies of Babylon, the esoteric traditions passed through the intellectual hubs of Cairo and Kairouan in North Africa, and subsequently entered southern Europe via Sicily and Muslim Spain. Throughout the early medieval period, this transmission route kept the esoteric binitarian flame alive, slowly integrating it with Neoplatonic concepts while secretly retaining the core structural dyad of the Prime Mover and the Active Intermediary.

The ultimate return and formalization of this suppressed binitarianism occurred in the Kabbalistic traditions of Spain and Provence, particularly within the _Zohar_. The strict monotheism of the Mishnaic era was effectively bypassed by the structural separation between the entirely unknowable _Ein Sof_ (the Infinite) and the emanated, dynamic _Sephirot_, particularly focusing on the mediating figure of the _Shekhinah_ or _Metatron_. Reformulating a classic Talmudic anecdote (Hagigah 15a) regarding the heretic Elisha ben Abuyah (Acher) who glimpsed this reality, we find the core binitarian scandal: 

#pad(left: 2em)[
_"Acher gazed into the heavenly realm and saw Kusapo, to whom authority was given to sit and write the merits of Israel. He said: 'Heaven forbid! Perhaps there are two powers in heaven: Tukamo and Kusapo!'"_ 
]

== 5. Islamic implicit Binitarianism: the _Haqiqat al-Muhammadiyya_

The binitarian impulse also permeated Islam, operating beneath its fiercely unitarian exterior (_Tawhid_). This implicit binitarianism is most fully articulated in Sufi metaphysics through the concepts of the _Haqiqat al-Muhammadiyya_ (the Muhammadan Reality) and the _Al-Insan al-Kamil_ (the Perfect Man). In this framework, the _Haqiqat al-Muhammadiyya_ functions as the pre-existent Logos—the first creation and the active principle through which the hidden, transcendent God becomes known to the cosmos. The Prophet Muhammad, understood in his cosmic rather than merely historical dimension, serves as the perfect mirror reflecting the divine attributes, structurally identical to the Son/Logos in Christian theology or Nabu in Babylon. Furthermore, this dynamic is inherently eschatological, projecting into the future via the theology of the _Mahdi_, who represents the final culmination of this active principle reordering the world at the end of time.

This dynamic is perfectly encapsulated in the famous Hadith Qudsi of the Hidden Treasure, heavily utilized by Ibn Arabi and the Sufi tradition. Reformulated, it reads: 

#pad(left: 2em)[
  _"Tukamo said: 'I was a hidden treasure and I loved to be known. Therefore, I created Kusapo, that I might be known.'"_
]

In this profound declaration, Kusapo is established as the indispensable, active epistemic bridge—the emergent process through which the unapproachable reality of Tukamo is finally made manifest and compiled within the created order.

= Conclusion

== 1. Road Not Taken 1: Early Jewish-Christian Binitarianism and the _Pneumatomachia_

This subsection provides a historical-critical survey of early pre-Nicene Binitarianism. It investigates early Jewish-Christian "Two Powers in Heaven" paradigms, early Logos theology, and worship practices that initially favored a strictly dyadic understanding of the Godhead. We analyze the historical and philosophical inflection points that led to the triumph of the Trinitarian synthesis, treating the suppression of Binitarianism as a profound "road not taken" in the structural development of Christian dogma.

A critical component of this historical analysis involves a deep reassessment of the fourth-century _Pneumatomachia_ movement. Conventionally dismissed merely as "Spirit-fighters" or an obstinate heresy lingering on the fringes of the Nicene settlement, the Pneumatomachian resistance to declaring the Holy Spirit a fully distinct, co-equal hypostasis warrants re-evaluation. Rather than a malicious denigration of the divine, their hesitation often stemmed from a rigorous biblicism and a deep reluctance to impose non-scriptural, philosophical terminology onto the Godhead. This subsection will argue that the Pneumatomachians represented the last, gasping defense of an older, functionally Binitarian worldview—a profound "road not taken" that, if successfully integrated into systematic theology, might have spared the Western church centuries of scholastic overcomplication and laid a much firmer foundation for the later Reformation.

== 2. Road Not Taken 2: Latent Binitarianism in Reformed Orthodoxy

Moving into the post-Reformation era, this section examines the rigid scholasticization of Calvinism. It argues that historical Calvinism, while maintaining a robust theology of absolute sovereignty, frequently operated with a functionally Binitarian praxis. The proposal investigates why early Reformed scholastics defaulted to inherited Nicene categories without revisiting the structural possibilities of a Binitarian Godhead, highlighting this as a missed opportunity to provide a cleaner teleological basis for doctrines of absolute divine sovereignty. As in section I.1., we ask: what would have happened if Calvin had chosen an overt Binitarian path after the 1537 debate with Caroli? Would he have been marginalized into a heretical footnote to the Reformation era? Maybe not.

== 3. The Road Ahead: Salvation from the Nietzschean Abyss

We argue here that contemporary Western culture is increasingly trapped in a myriad of "Nietzschean hyper-individualist death cults." Having assassinated God to emancipate the self, modern humanity finds itself adrift in a crisis of meaning and gasping for spiritual air, ultimately preferring the "Kool-Aid" of individual and collective suicide over the redemptive submission required by Vertical Transcendence. Without a transcendent anchor, the immanent realm collapses into a chaotic war of all against all, driven by mimetic rivalry and the idolatrous worship of pseudo-autonomous monsters. Consequently, the rejection of the Christian understanding of reality does not lead to liberation, but to a pathological self-destruction where humanity actively wills its own negation rather than bow down to the Father.

This cultural death cult pattern is starkly exemplified by contemporary archetypes of predatory sovereignty, oscillating between grim reality and mass-market fantasy, such as Jeffrey Epstein and the fictional Christian Grey (the eponymous anti-hero of the _Fifty Shades_ franchise). These figures validate René Girard's profound insights regarding ontological sickness: the inevitable descent from vain autonomy into frigidity, the popular rise of BDSM, and the manifestation of Stavrogin-esque pedophilia, the ultimate _aversio a Deo_ burning of bridges to the Deity. When the soul is starved of actual transcendence, it attempts to violently extract meaning and being from others through domination and sexual transgression.

The underlying mechanics of this death cult logic are equally apparent in the intellectual trajectory of postmodernism, most notably in the life and work of Michel Foucault. While Foucault ostensibly dedicated his career to the critical deconstruction and exposure of power structures, this project ultimately reveals itself as an inverted form of power worship. In exact Girardian fashion, Foucault's intellectual craving for power materialized somatically in his personal life—philosophically justified as a pursuit of "limit-experiences" and transgressive liberation—culminating in a desire to physically experience absolute domination, or, to speak plainly, a desire to feel the literal sexual subjection to an imaginary "power". Foucault’s sophisticated philosophical defenses of these "experiences" ultimately served merely as intellectual excuses and justifications for this base mimetic craving. We will argue that this was not merely a biographical quirk, but the logical terminus of his philosophy: unable to locate Vertical Transcendence and submit to it, the postmodern subject seeks the absolute within extreme immanent degradation. This trajectory inevitably resulted in the broader intellectual suicide that Postmodernism (PoMo) conducted within the humanities and spread into broader society. 

It seems to us that in our time, spiritual-intellectual life in the West is corseted, suffocated yet fascinated by the dark glare of the hyper-Nietzschean anti-transcendent individual; the infinite Legion#footnote[Mark 5:1–20. See also the Dostoevskian usage of this passage as the incipit of _Demons_ and the analysis of that use by Girard in _DD&TN_] of ever more sacred yet shrunken atoms. It is the Moloch of the day, who demands that everything and everyone be sacrificed to him. By contrast, in the theology just developed, there is only one genuine individual, the _monogenes_ Kusapo; the ultimate expression of counter-Nietzschean dissent. As always with Christianity, the path toward peace and _agape_ passes through the narrow door of emancipatory submission to the incarnate but transcendent Logos.